let's be honest for many the thought of  cycling in a city is terrifying  you could get bored or hit by a bus if  you were cyclists in New York City in  the 2000 it was almost like you were a  cast member from Escape from New York we  people were maybe an extreme sport  person it was fantastic for you because  you were dodging cars it was you know  not a place to have you know any kind of  safe commuting regular transport  experience but today New York City is  seeing explosive growth in cycle  commutes so how did the city with  traffic like this get so many people  biking downtown  from 2007 to 2013 jeanette Sadiq Khan  led New York City's Department of  Transportation I was in charge of 6000  miles of streets 789 bridges 12,000  signalized intersections 1.3 million  street signs  we put down 400 miles of on-street bike  lanes including one of the first parking  protected bike lanes in the United  States and we did it on 9th Avenue  between 23rd Street and 16th Street this  Lane was significant because it was an  early parking protected design it's much  less stressful than conventional lanes  but installing it was quite  controversial because making the space  for protected lanes meant the  elimination of parking taking away  parking spaces is not for the faint of  heart and there is no super-secret you  know magic recipe that's going to make  it easier to do but you need to make the  case about what you are trying to do  right the ninth Avenue pilot ran for six  blocks and if you look at the blueprint  for the street you'll notice an  alternating pattern on blocks with  right-hand turns there's an entire lane  of parking  but at left turns those parking spots  are sacrificed for a barrier the  vehicles will wait next to the bike lane  here and separate signals for cars and  bikes stager to reduce the stress at the  intersection data show that the 9th  avenue bike lane produced economic  mobility and safety benefits we saw  crashes go down from 47% retail sales  went up almost 49% cars had dedicated  turn lanes so the traffic process much  better and bikes had a dedicated lane so  it was a win for business it was a win  for drivers was a win for people on foot  and it was winning for people on two  wheels and that really set the stage for  all that followed once the city analyzed  data from the 9th Avenue pile  the miles of protected Lane in New York  skyrocketed by 2018 the city had nearly  1,200 miles of bike lane and 100 of  those were protected because we weren't  going to accommodate a million more  people by just double decking our  streets and highways and so designing  streets for people that they get easier  to buy it and easier to walk it easier  to take the bus  that's the kind of recipe for future  success of cities and the cities that  make these kinds of investments and  changes are the cities that are growing  and thriving in the century but to  really draw a crowd of cyclists a city  needs a network of low stress bike lanes  you can't just paint sharrows on a  street and expect that people are gonna  while lost start biking it needs to be a  reliable system and it needs to be safe  and the way that we look at the health  of our bike lanes and a bike lane  network is how many women and children  are using the lanes because when when  you see women and kids in the lanes and  families in the lanes you know that it's  safe families among other individuals  would fall into the interested but  concerned group on this chart it's from  a survey taken in Oregon but consensus  in the transportation field suggests  that this group is the untapped  potential for cities who want to promote  cycling which is the big factor behind  New York's boom in urban cyclists and it  was pretty cost-effective you know the  bike lanes were like 99% of our  headlines but they were only 1% of the  budget so I don't think there's a better  investment if you want to build a better  city you can start by building bike  lanes  [Music]
 

